"Scope: the task text contained only the end of the design doc (refs 99\u2013106 and Appendices A\u2013D). The main body and refs 1\u201398 were not included, so findings about main-body claims are inferred from Appendices B\u2013D and from the doc's own scripts and bench (Appendix C), which I ran. The web-search budget ran out partway through. Citations were checked by fetching the sources directly (ITU, Crossref, arXiv/ISCA, pyroomacoustics docs, sherpa-onnx, Hugging Face, GitHub) and against the author's saved Crossref records.\n\n1. **The two-talker beam spec (r_A=+1, r_B=\u22121; tests B.1 and B.7) is wrong.**\n   - The doc's own phase search in physics_check.py returns \u03c8(f) = 190/200/222/265/312/348\u00b0 at 0.25/0.5/1/2/3.15/4 kHz (4 cm). That is exactly 180\u00b0+360\u00b0\u00b7f\u00b7d/c, i.e. the equalized figure-8 with r_B = \u2212e^{j\u03c9d/c}.\n   - Forcing r_B = \u22121 is infeasible against the WNG floor. Dipole WNG = sin\u00b2\u03c6/(1+cos\u00b2\u03c6) with \u03c6 = 2\u03c0fd/c, which is \u221216 dB at 300 Hz for 4 cm.\n   - With a \u221210 dB floor, B.1's \u00b11 dB check can only pass at 962\u20135898 Hz (2.5 cm), 601\u20133686 Hz (4 cm) and 401\u20132457 Hz (6 cm). At 6 cm, \"below 3 kHz\" also crosses c/2d = 2858 Hz.\n   - With the floor strictly enforced, the fixed \u22121 beam gives AI-DI of 0.9/\u22120.4/\u22121.6 dB (3D noise, 2.5/4/6 cm). That is worse than one omni mic at 4\u20136 cm; at 6 cm both partners are notched by \u22128.7 dB at 2.5 kHz and \u221214.3 dB at 3.15 kHz. The phase-optimized beam gives 3.4/3.6/3.5 dB.\n   - Fix:\n     - Specify \u03c8(f) = 180\u00b0+\u03c9d\u0302/c in the mid band, rotating smoothly toward the omni-like solution where the floor binds.\n     - Never amplitude-blend the dipole with omni: omni plus figure-8 is a cardioid, which nulls partner B in the crossover band.\n     - Rewrite B.7 as phase continuity (\u226430\u00b0) of each partner's response across states A/AB/B.\n     - Limit B.1 to floor-feasible bins below 0.8\u00b7c/2d\u0302.\n   - Also, physics_check.two_talker() silently returns weights that violate the floor when its bisection fails. With the floor enforced, its 3.82/3.81/3.67 dB become 3.42/3.60/3.50 dB.\n\n2. **Bus metrics B1\u2013B7 must be scored per partner and tolerate polarity/phase differences.** The current score_bus uses one summed reference, and align() applies one global sign.\n   - Measured on bus_s0_snr+0_d40mm: an ideal noise-free output with partner B inverted scores dSISDR \u22122.6 dB (vs +235 with the same polarity), ESTOI 0.80, wrstT 0.75 and target gain \u22129.4 dB.\n   - With noise at \u221210 dB: dSISDR \u22124.3 vs +10.1, and BR +0.7 vs +10.1.\n   - Fix:\n     - Store separate early images for each partner.\n     - Score each partner with an allowed-distortion FIR (BSS-Eval-style SDR, ~512 taps) or with magnitude-domain metrics.\n     - Remove the global sign flip.\n\n3. **Replace metrics that reward gating or over-suppressing far talkers.**\n   - Bus BR is measured only in target-silent regions. An oracle-VAD gate on raw Mic A, with the noise under speech untouched, scores BR +40 dB (the cap) while ESTOI is unchanged at 0.381.\n   - Meeting:\n     - gapSNR rewards gating.\n     - dSISDR and ESTOI are scale-invariant, so far talkers losing level before AGC is invisible.\n     - loud\u03c3 is measured on the noisy output, so an AGC that re-boosts a suppressed far talker (and its noise floor) looks \"even\".\n     - The median hides the far talkers.\n   - The doc's own cdr_check.py shows why this matters. The CDR post-filter (\u03bc 1.3, Gmin 0.1, \u03bb 0.68, 4 cm) cuts a DRR \u22128 dB meeting talker by 5.6 dB, and by 6.2 dB in 1\u20134 kHz. That is more than it cuts the noise (1\u20134 kHz dSNR \u22120.8 dB); with \u03bb 0.9 the cut is 9 dB.\n   - Required:\n     - Hagerman-separated target and noise components for each talker.\n     - Noise attenuation during speech reported separately from idle periods (Teams \"idle/active channel SpNR\" [103]; ITU-T G.160 App. II TNLR/NPLR/SNRI/DSN).\n     - Per-talker attenuation before AGC and level after AGC (Teams \"speech level for alternating near-end talkers\" and \"quiet speech\" tests).\n     - Distance bins (<2 / 2\u20134 / >4 m) plus worst talker.\n     - Noise-floor pumping across talker turns.\n     - Per-talker WER from a small ONNX ASR; the corpus manifest already has transcripts.\n     - A gate such as: far-bin attenuation before AGC \u2264 near-bin + 3 dB.\n     - Do not tune on DNSMOS BAK/OVRL, because BAK rewards silence.\n\n4. **The bus simulation is unrealistically easy.**\n   - Partners are drawn from 0.3\u20130.8 m but passengers are always \u22651.0 m away; the brief says partners 0.3\u20131 m. This creates an artificial guard band.\n   - Passengers speak at 60\u201368 dB SPL@1 m while partners are Lombard at 66\u201370. Passengers in the same noise would also raise their voices.\n   - Partners are always on opposite sides of the device (\u00b140\u00b0), which is the only geometry a 2-mic figure-8 handles well.\n   - All sources are omnidirectional.\n   - The late reverb tail and road noise are generated with exactly the sinc coherence that the CDR/MVDR models assume. In Schwarz & Kellermann Fig. 5, real tails deviate from that.\n   - There is no handling, vibration or wind noise.\n   - Add:\n     - 1\u20132 conversing interferers at 0.6\u20131.2 m at partner level.\n     - A side-by-side seated pair, and a device lying flat in a lap (partners above it, so \u03c4\u22480 for both).\n     - Source directivity (supported only with image-source simulation in shoebox rooms).\n     - Real 2-channel noise: DEMAND TBUS/PCAFETER mic pairs at 5 cm, or ETSI TS 103 224 \"Inside Bus\".\n   - Report leakage from the nearest interferers separately from distant babble.\n\n5. **State the 2-mic physical ceiling in \u00a71 and drop cues that don't work.**\n   - Two-talker beam against horizontal (2D) babble: 2.2\u20132.3 dB AI-DI, and only when the partners are near the array axis; about 0 dB at broadside.\n   - Steered single-target MVDR, averaged over azimuth in 2D noise: 2.5\u20132.7 dB, so critical distance grows \u00d71.34\u20131.37, not the \"hypercardioid \u00d72\" figure. At broadside it is 0.8\u20132.0 dB. \u00b11 dB/\u00b13\u00b0 mic mismatch costs \u22640.1 dB at this floor.\n   - CDR post-filter for a near partner: only +1.2\u20132.0 dB broadband dSNR.\n   - Near-field ILD: \u22641.6 dB at 0.3 m (6 cm, endfire). That is below calibration and reverb error, so it cannot serve as a distance cue.\n   - Say explicitly that most of the gain must come from single-channel noise reduction, WPE, levelling and speaker cues.\n\n6. **Make orientation a controlled test factor and a user instruction.**\n   - Partner angles off the array axis in the unique bus geometries: dev s0 21\u00b0/38\u00b0, s1 78\u00b0/88\u00b0 (both near broadside), s2 74\u00b0/49\u00b0; test s10 46\u00b0/56\u00b0, s11 10\u00b0/4\u00b0, s12 15\u00b0/9\u00b0.\n   - In s1 the partners have nearly identical TDOAs and the figure-8 nulls both.\n   - Dev and test therefore differ systematically, and G1/G2 decisions can flip on orientation luck.\n   - Stratify scenes as aligned / 45\u00b0 / broadside.\n   - Add a \"point the mic axis through the two partners\" rule, plus a fallback for when both partners' |\u03c4| < ~0.3\u00b7d/c.\n   - For the meeting, recommend the mic axis along the table's long axis. Then |\u03c4| tracks talker distance (Spearman 0.98, vs 0.06 for random azimuth), which allows a distance-compensating gain indexed by TDOA.\n\n7. **Meeting: two mics cannot tell 20 talkers apart by TDOA.**\n   - At 4 cm the 20 seats collapse to a median of 8 TDOA groups at 20 \u00b5s resolution (12 at 10 \u00b5s; 6 for 2.5 cm).\n   - 8\u201313 of 19 neighbouring seats differ by less than 10 \u00b5s, and mirror-image seats share a TDOA but not a room response.\n   - M10(c)'s \"per-cluster memory\" (and any AGC or [55] memory keyed by TDOA) must key on speaker embeddings, with |\u03c4| as a secondary cue.\n   - make_meeting has each talker speak exactly once, so per-talker memory is never exercised. Regenerate scenes with returning talkers, back-channels and interruptions, lasting at least 5\u201310 minutes.\n\n8. **Meeting simulation geometry.**\n   - The device floats at 0.78 m in a shoebox with no table. The dominant tabletop reflection is missing, while a floor reflection that the table would block is present.\n   - The farthest talker in every scene is 4.6\u20134.85 m, against the 6 m requirement.\n   - There is no standing presenter facing away, and all talkers are omnidirectional.\n   - Add an explicit table image source, extend talkers to 6 m and add directivity.\n   - Recommend mounting the mics within ~2 cm of the table surface. At 10 cm or 20 cm height, a talker 3 m away produces a comb notch near 6.2 kHz or 3.1 kHz.\n\n9. **Replace TTS speech before selecting any method.**\n   - The corpus is macOS `say` output. 4 of the 12 bus target voices (Fred, Kathy, Ralph, Junior) are formant synthesizers, and the babble is non-English TTS voices reading English.\n   - The NN models, DNSMOS and ESTOI are trained or calibrated on natural speech.\n   - Move LibriSpeech or a conversational corpus to Week 1, and decide G1 on natural speech.\n\n10. **Too few scenes to support decisions.**\n    - The dev set is 13 bus scenes from 3 geometries and 5 meeting scenes from 3 rooms. The test set is 3 geometries + 3 rooms.\n    - Use \u226530 independent stratified geometries per scenario.\n    - Report paired-bootstrap 95% CIs, and define G1/G2 as improvements whose CI excludes zero.\n    - Seed pyroomacoustics' global RNG with pra.random.seed(); the randomized image-source jitter is currently unseeded, so regenerated scenes differ.\n\n11. **CDR spec: test B.2 fails as written.**\n    - With the doc's own settings (prop3, \u03bb 0.68, 4 cm), pure diffuse noise gives a median short-time CDR of \u22120.4 to \u22120.8 dB, with 45\u201347% of bins above 0 dB.\n    - \u03bc/Gmin/\u03bb were tuned by Schwarz & Kellermann at d = 8 cm. At 2.5 cm and 4 cm, a broadside talker has |\u0393s\u2212\u0393n| = 0.03 and 0.09 at 1 kHz, so the estimator is ill-conditioned there.\n    - Restate B.2 as a long-term CDR test, plus \"short-time power-averaged gain \u2264 \u22128 dB\".\n    - Apply the CDR gain only where |\u0393s\u2212\u0393n| \u2265 ~0.2, retune for 2\u20136 cm, and use WPE rather than CDR for meeting dereverberation.\n\n12. **S0/S1 calibration model is too narrow.**\n    - S0 recovers only a scalar gain and delay. Spread in the INMP441's ~60 Hz low-frequency roll-off causes frequency-dependent phase mismatch, so S0 needs a per-band complex correction.\n    - The spacing estimate from the diffuse fit is biased:\n      - Self-noise caps |\u03b3| at about 0.83\u20130.94 in a 40\u201345 dBA room, which inflates the fitted spacing.\n      - The sinc-vs-J0 model choice alone causes ~25% spacing error.\n      - Fit \u03c1\u00b7\u0393(d) with a free \u03c1.\n    - Make the primary procedure calipers plus a chirp played from each endfire side. \u03c4\u00b1 = \u00b1d/c + \u03b4 gives d = c(\u03c4\u208a\u2212\u03c4\u208b)/2 and the I2S skew \u03b4 = (\u03c4\u208a+\u03c4\u208b)/2, which settles the 31.25 \u00b5s half-frame question.\n\n13. **Hagerman and latency harness.**\n    - finalize() adds sensor noise to the mix but never stores it. comp2_* must include the sensor noise and per-mic gains, or the s\u00b1n re-synthesis changes the scene.\n    - B.6's impulse probe is invalid for NN, mask-based and adaptive methods. Measure delay by cross-correlating the Hagerman-separated s_out with the reference on speech.\n    - Report algorithmic latency separately: 32 ms for the 512-point STFT, a 20 ms window/10 ms hop for DPDFNet, and ~24 ms plus chunk for VoxLock.\n\n14. **M8/M9 cannot be implemented as written.**\n    - NSNet2 is not in sherpa-onnx's speech-enhancement release, which only has GTCRN, DPDFNet and VoxLock. It is also not on DNS-Challenge master. So \"validate NSNet2 against the sherpa-onnx reference\" cannot be done; pin the NSNet2 source and its reference script instead.\n    - VoxLock (M9) extracts a single target and auto-selects the dominant voice in the first 15 s. On the bus it would remove partner B.\n    - Running it twice with two enrollments costs ~1.5 RTF single-threaded, which breaks the bench's RTF < 0.5 unless parallelized.\n    - Gate self-enrollment on near-field confidence and measure a wrong-speaker-lock rate.\n\n15. **Missing scope and industry practice.**\n    - State whether the meeting device is capture-only. If far-end audio plays in the room, AEC and double-talk handling are mandatory; the cited Teams spec tests both.\n    - Move the ESP32 calibration and real recordings from Week 4 to Week 1, since sim-to-real mismatch is the main risk [104].\n    - Optional hardware path: if the ESP32-C3's I2S supports TDM (confirm), a 3\u20134-mic TDM array would remove the 2-mic front-back ambiguity for the meeting case.\n    - Specify the listening test as ITU-T P.835.\n\n16. **Citations and text.**\n    - [99]: the URL's file name, Taal2011_1.pdf, points to the 2011 STOI paper, not ESTOI. The host refused the connection, so the content is unverified. Use doi:10.1109/TASLP.2016.2585878.\n    - [106]: add pp. 5003\u20135007 and doi:10.21437/Interspeech.2024-1788.\n    - [102]: add the Husstedt et al. title.\n    - Appendix D: the randomized image-source method removes \"sweeping echoes\", not \"flutter echoes\".\n    - Appendix D, CHiME-3: the 6-mic tablet frame (mic 2 rear-facing; neighbour spacing \u224810 cm, recalled not confirmed) does not match a 2.5\u20136 cm pair, and its WSJ0-based content is LDC-licensed. Prefer DEMAND, which has 5 cm pairs.\n    - The author's saved Crossref record shows DOI 10.1109/ASPAA.1995.482964 is Kulkarni et al. (HRTF), not Elko & Pong. Check whether refs 1\u201398 use it.\n    - Crossref holds only the 2011 early-access record for Yousefian & Loizou. Cite the final print version (I believe 20(2):599\u2013609, 2012; not confirmed).\n    - Verified correct: [100] (P.862 family deleted 5 Jan 2024), [101], [103], [104], [105], and pyroomacoustics 0.10.1.\n\nProbe scripts (run with speech_lab/.venv/bin/python -I, passing the speech_lab path or the review folder as the argument):\n/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-chpatel-Library-Application-Support-Claude-scratch-workspaces-7223bc40-0755-4c47-a940-84a904b8ead4-809a743c-126a-440a-9a06-f59818e63020-scratch-2026-11-02-5e756e/c3614d02-9a38-4a14-8dcb-766e8c2e70cf/scratchpad/review/probe.py\n/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-chpatel-Library-Application-Support-Claude-scratch-workspaces-7223bc40-0755-4c47-a940-84a904b8ead4-809a743c-126a-440a-9a06-f59818e63020-scratch-2026-11-02-5e756e/c3614d02-9a38-4a14-8dcb-766e8c2e70cf/scratchpad/review/di_real.py\n/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-chpatel-Library-Application-Support-Claude-scratch-workspaces-7223bc40-0755-4c47-a940-84a904b8ead4-809a743c-126a-440a-9a06-f59818e63020-scratch-2026-11-02-5e756e/c3614d02-9a38-4a14-8dcb-766e8c2e70cf/scratchpad/review/fig8c.py\n/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-chpatel-Library-Application-Support-Claude-scratch-workspaces-7223bc40-0755-4c47-a940-84a904b8ead4-809a743c-126a-440a-9a06-f59818e63020-scratch-2026-11-02-5e756e/c3614d02-9a38-4a14-8dcb-766e8c2e70cf/scratchpad/review/axis.py"